course: books
lesson: 65
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XI, part 1: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears» (home at the Rat's, a telling-off, and bad news)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Rat grips Toad by the scruff of the neck and hauls him up into his hall, dripping with mud and weed. At once Toad starts boasting: prison, escape, disguises, the canal, the stolen horse — «Oh, I am a smart Toad, and no mistake!» The Rat sends him straight upstairs to take off «that old cotton rag» and come down looking like a gentleman, «if you can».
explain: Toad wants to argue, but he catches sight of himself in the mirror, the rusty black bonnet perched over one eye, and goes upstairs quickly and humbly. Washed and in the Rat's clothes, he admires himself and wonders how anybody could ever have taken him for a washerwoman.
explain: Over lunch Toad tells all his adventures as if they were splendid fun, and the Rat grows more and more grave and silent. Then the Rat speaks seriously: Toad has been handcuffed, starved, chased, jeered at and flung into the water — and all because he must steal a motor-car. Why be a convict? Does Toad think the Rat enjoys hearing that he «keeps company with gaol-birds»?
explain: Inside, Toad is still muttering «But it was fun, though! Awful fun!», but out loud he says sweetly, «Quite right, Ratty! How sound you always are!» He promises a quiet life at Toad Hall — after a quick mention of a «brilliant idea» about motor-boats that makes the Rat stamp. Then the Rat cries: hasn't he heard? The Stoats and Weasels have taken Toad Hall! A large tear rolls down each of Toad's cheeks, plop! plop!
fact: The chapter's title, «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears», comes, changed a little, from a line in Tennyson's poem «The Princess»; a summer tempest is a sudden storm that soon passes — like Toad's quick tears.
fact: The Rat «gripped Toad firmly by the scruff of the neck» (the loose skin at the back of the neck) and hauled him in over the edge of the hole.
fact: Toad boasts: «Been in prison—got out of it, of course! Been thrown into a canal—swam ashore! Stole a horse—sold him for a large sum of money! Humbugged everybody».
fact: The Rat: «Take off that old cotton rag that looks as if it might formerly have belonged to some washerwoman... and try and come down looking like a gentleman if you can».
fact: Toad sees himself in the looking-glass over the hat-stand, «with the rusty black bonnet perched rakishly over one eye», and goes upstairs «very quickly and humbly» (rakishly = at a careless, cheeky angle).
fact: «The more he talked and boasted, the more grave and silent the Rat became.» (grave = serious)
fact: The Rat lists what Toad has been: «handcuffed, imprisoned, starved, chased, terrified out of your life, insulted, jeered at, and ignominiously flung into the water—by a woman, too!» (ignominiously = shamefully)
fact: «Where's the amusement in that? Where does the fun come in? And all because you must needs go and steal a motor-car.» (must needs = just had to) He adds that Toad has «never had anything but trouble from motor-cars».
fact: The Rat's sharpest point: «why choose to be a convict?» and «Do you suppose it's any pleasure to me... to hear animals saying... that I'm the chap that keeps company with gaol-birds?» (a gaol-bird = someone often in prison; gaol = jail)
fact: Toad «never minded being jawed by those who were his real friends» (to jaw = to tell off at length) and could always «see the other side of the question».
fact: Inside he mutters «But it was fun, though! Awful fun!» and makes stifled noises «k-i-ck-ck-ck, and poop-p-p», like snorts or soda-water bottles opening.
fact: Out loud: «Quite right, Ratty! How sound you always are! Yes, I've been a conceited old ass... but now I'm going to be a good Toad» (sound = wise, sensible).
fact: Toad's «brilliant idea—connected with motor-boats» makes the Rat «stamp, and upset things»; then Toad plans a quiet life of «landscape gardening», «a bit of dinner for my friends» and «a pony-chaise» (a light carriage pulled by a pony).
fact: «Do you mean to tell me... that you've heard nothing about the Stoats and Weasels? ... And how they've been and taken Toad Hall?»
fact: Toad cries, «plop! plop!», then says: «Go on, Ratty... tell me all. The worst is over. I am an animal again. I can bear it.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «our kid» (Manchester) = my brother or sister; «sound» (Liverpool) = good, fine; «it's giving» = it looks like; «no cap» = honestly; «wee» (Scotland) = little.
exercise: How did the Rat pull Toad out of the water? | By the scruff of the neck | By his bonnet strings | With a rope tied to a tree beside the hole | With a boat-hook
exercise: How did Toad feel once he was safe in the Rat's hall? | Happy and high-spirited as of old | Ashamed and silent | Ill and shivering, and he went straight to bed for three whole days | Angry with the Rat
exercise: «Oh, I am a smart Toad, and no mistake!» What was Toad doing? | Boasting about all his adventures | Thanking the Rat | Apologising to Rat | Crying about the stolen horse and promising to pay the barge-woman back
exercise: What did the Rat tell Toad to take off? | That old cotton rag of a washerwoman's dress | His wet boots | His gold chain | The heavy chains the policemen had put on him
exercise: Whose clothes did Toad put on? | The Rat's | Mole's old ones | His own, from Toad Hall | The washerwoman's spare dress, which was dry
exercise: What made Toad stop arguing and go upstairs humbly? | He saw himself in the mirror with the rusty bonnet over one eye | Rat hit him | He was much too cold and wet and tired to argue with anybody | Rat promised him lunch
exercise: «Perched rakishly over one eye.» What does «rakishly» mean? | At a careless, cheeky angle | Very neatly | Upside down | Tied tightly with a ribbon, like a parcel
exercise: What did Toad think about the people he had fooled? | What idiots they were to take him for a washerwoman | That they were very kind | That they had been cleverer than him all along and only pretended | That he owed every one of them an apology
exercise: «The more he talked and boasted, the more ...» what did the Rat become? | Grave and silent | Excited | Sleepy | Angry, so that he shouted and banged on the table all through lunch
exercise: «Ignominiously flung into the water—by a woman, too!» What does «ignominiously» mean? | Shamefully | Bravely, like a hero | Quickly, all at once | By accident, without anybody meaning it
exercise: What did the Rat say Toad has never had from motor-cars? | Anything but trouble | A fair price | A good ride | A single friend, except the gentlemen with the pond
exercise: «Why choose to be a ...» what, asks the Rat? | Convict | Washerwoman | Bankrupt | Gentleman with a pony-chaise
exercise: What does the Rat hate hearing other animals say? | That he keeps company with gaol-birds | That he writes bad poems | That his boat leaks | That he is afraid of the weasels in the Wild Wood
exercise: «Never minded being jawed by those who were his real friends.» What does «jawed» mean? | Told off at length | Bitten on the nose by the Rat | Laughed at by everyone | Given lots of food to chew
exercise: What was Toad muttering to himself during the lecture? | «But it was fun, though! Awful fun!» | «I'm sorry, I'm sorry» | «Poor old Ratty, he doesn't understand anything at all» | «Toad's luck again!»
exercise: What strange noises did Toad make inside him? | «k-i-ck-ck-ck» and «poop-p-p» | Ducks' quacks | Hiccups, from eating his lunch far too fast | A whistle, like the train's
exercise: «Quite right, Ratty! How sound you always are!» What does «sound» mean here? | Wise and sensible | Making a loud noise | Fast asleep and dreaming | Healthy, with no colds
exercise: What «brilliant idea» did Toad nearly start talking about? | Motor-boats | Aeroplanes in the sky | Big canal barges | A brand-new caravan, painted red, with a bird-cage
exercise: What did the Rat do when he heard «motor-boats»? | Stamped and upset things | Laughed | Fainted | Wrote it down in his notebook as a good plan
exercise: Which of these was NOT part of Toad's plan for a quiet life? | Racing motor-cars | Landscape gardening | A neat pony-chaise for trips | Dinners for friends
exercise: «Stroll quietly down to Toad Hall?» Why couldn't Toad do that? | The Stoats and Weasels had taken Toad Hall | It had burnt down while he was away in prison | The police were waiting there | Badger had sold it to pay Toad's fines
exercise: How did Toad first react to the news? | A large tear in each eye, plop! plop! | He laughed | He ran off at once | He fainted on the floor and Rat had to fetch water
exercise: «I am an animal again. I can bear it.» What does Toad mean? | He is himself again, not a washerwoman, and can face bad news | He has turned back from a bird | He is brave enough to fight | He is hungry and wants his supper straight away
test: Ruby, it's giving (it looks like) drama: «the Rat was well chuffed to see Toad, gave him a hero's welcome.» Put it right. | Rat helped him but sent him upstairs at once and later told him off seriously | Right, Rat clapped and cheered | Rat refused to let him in and pushed him back into the river | Rat had never met Toad before
test: Why does the Rat say «looking like a gentleman if you can»? | Toad, a rich gentleman, looks a shabby, muddy, dripping mess | Toad was never a gentleman, he was always a poor washerwoman | Rat is jealous of his clothes | Rat is joking about his singing
test: What does the mirror scene show about Toad? | Even Toad can see how silly he looks — and that changes his mind fast | Toad is afraid of mirrors ever since he saw the castle's ghosts | Toad likes the bonnet best of all his hats | Toad cannot see without glasses
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad really agrees with every word the Rat says.» | Partly false: inside he still thinks «it was fun», but out loud he agrees | True: he is fully cured and agrees with every single word | False: he shouts at the Rat | True: he cries all through lunch
test: Josh, no cap (honestly): «Rat's speech was savage, Toad hated him after.» What does the book say? | Toad never minded being told off by his real friends | Toad left the house in a huff and never spoke to the Rat again | Toad threw his lunch at him | Toad called the police
test: Why is Toad's «quiet, steady, respectable life» promise hard to believe? | He mentions a new motor-boat idea in the same breath | He has never promised anything before in his whole life, so nobody trusts him | He hates gardening | Rat says it is true
test: What would probably have happened if Toad had strolled to Toad Hall without hearing the news? | He would have walked straight into the stoats and weasels | He would have had a nice quiet tea | The police would have been kind | Badger would have been waiting there with a hot supper ready
test: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears.» Why is this a good title for this chapter? | Toad's tears burst out suddenly and are over just as fast, like a summer storm | Toad gets caught in a thunderstorm on the way to the Rat's house | It rains all chapter | Toad is crying about the weather
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what other «fads» had Toad taken up before? | Sailing, punting and house-boating | Painting pictures, singing songs and dancing at grand balls | Fishing and swimming | Gardening
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Badger told Toad he was «squandering the money your father left you». What does «squandering» mean? | Wasting | Saving | Hiding | Counting
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, in his dungeon, what did Toad call his friends? | «O wise old Badger! O clever, intelligent Rat and sensible Mole!» | «Stupid fellows» | «Traitors» | «My servants, who will be punished when I get out»
test: Looking back: in chapter IX, who stopped the Rat from going south with the Sea Rat? | The Mole | Toad, out of prison | Badger, from his wood | The swallows
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, the Rat said «Talking to Toad'll never cure him. He'll say anything.» How does this part prove him right? | Toad says «Quite right, Ratty!» while still thinking «it was fun» | Toad is cured completely by the Rat's long talking-to | Toad says nothing at all | Toad agrees with Badger only
test: Looking back: how is this rescue like the Mole's first day on the river in chapter I? | Both times the Rat pulls a wet, sorry animal out of the river and looks after him | Both times Toad rows the boat and splashes everyone | Both times it snows | Both times Badger comes
test: Liam, our kid (my brother), says: «Toad cried 'cos Rat was mean.» Why did Toad really cry? | He heard that the Wild Wooders had taken his home | Rat hit him | He lost his money | He missed the barge-woman and her kind husband
test: ★★★ The Rat lists what has happened to Toad: «handcuffed, imprisoned, starved, chased, terrified out of your life, insulted, jeered at, and ... flung into the water». How many things is that? | 8 | 6 | 7 | 9
test: ★★★ Toad says «The worst is over» BEFORE he hears the full story. Why is that a trap for the reader? | The worst — the attack on Mole and Badger and the loss of the Hall — is still to be told | He has already heard everything | The worst was the canal, so nothing else can hurt him now | The worst was prison
exam: Identify the article of attire which the Water Rat directed Toad to remove upon his arrival. | The cotton gown formerly belonging to a washerwoman | His motoring goggles, cap, gaiters and gauntleted gloves | His wet boots and gaiters | The chains of his imprisonment
exam: Ascertain the circumstance that occasioned Toad's ready compliance with the Rat's instructions. | His beholding his own reflection in the looking-glass | The Rat's threat to summon the police and the magistrates | The arrival of Badger | The prospect of luncheon
exam: It is given that the Rat expressed displeasure at being thought to «keep company with gaol-birds». Select the response which most accurately characterises his objection to Toad's conduct. | That Toad had chosen to become a convict by stealing motor-cars | That Toad had sold a horse belonging to another | That Toad had neglected his boating and his duties upon the river | That Toad wore improper clothing at table
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the intelligence conveyed to Toad at the close of this part? | That the Stoats and Weasels had taken possession of Toad Hall | That Toad Hall had been sold to defray his fines | That Badger had departed the district | That the police awaited him at his residence
